<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charites</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Graces</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Charites, or Graces, were goddesses of grace, beauty and festivity in Greek religion, usually three in number: Aglaia, Euphrosyne and Thalia. Their oldest and most famous cult was at Orchomenus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>, where they were worshipped as stones said to have fallen from the sky and honoured with the Charitesia festival of music and poetry. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindar">Pindar</a>'s Fourteenth Olympian ode celebrates them.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charites.jpg" alt="Charites" width="320" height="368" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Three_Graces,_from_Pompeii_(fresco).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Charites, called the Graces in English and the Gratiae in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, personify beauty and grace. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a>'s Theogony they are the three daughters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> and the Oceanid Eurynome: Aglaia, Euphrosyne and Thalia. Accounts differ, however, in names, number and parentage. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> mentions Pasithea as one of the younger Graces, and Pausanias reports that the Athenians worshipped two Charites, Auxo and Hegemone, and the Spartans two, Cleta and Phaenna, while by Boeotian tradition Eteocles, king of Orchomenus, fixed the number at three.</p><p>They have little independent mythology and mostly attend the Olympian gods, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aphrodite">Aphrodite</a>, whom they bathed and anointed at Paphos. Pindar says they arranged feasts and dances for the Olympians and danced with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muses">Muses</a>. One of them was the wife of the smith god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephaestus">Hephaestus</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a> calls her Aglaia, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> she is called Charis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Their cult is very old, and their name appears to be pre-Greek in origin. Its purpose seems to have resembled that of the nymphs, concerned with fertility and nature and with springs and rivers. An inscription of the sixth century BCE on Thera records a cult, and dance was strongly linked with it. At least four temples were dedicated to them alone, at Orchomenus, Hermione, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> and Elis. The most important was at Orchomenus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>, where the cult was thought to have begun. There the goddesses were worshipped at a very ancient site with a trio of stones and received a share of the produce of the rich plain, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> says that Eteocles founded their temple.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In Greek art of the Archaic and Classical periods the Charites were dressed and shown in a line as dancers. The Roman and later pose, a nude interlaced group, developed later; Pausanias says he could not find who first showed them naked. The earliest known image is from a temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thermon">Thermon</a>, of the seventh or sixth century BCE. A Classical relief of the Charites and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermes">Hermes</a> at the entrance to the Acropolis was popularly ascribed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> the nude group allowed pagan beauty to appear in art, and the Graces were painted by Botticelli in Primavera and sculpted by Antonio Canova and Bertel Thorvaldsen.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charites” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its cult of the <b>Graces</b>, the Charites, was among the oldest in Greece.</blockquote><a href="/state/orchomenus-boeotia">Orchomenus (Boeotia)</a></li></ul></section>
